<p>A North Texas hailstorm can be over in ten minutes. The ice melts, the sun comes out, and by the next morning it can be hard to believe anything happened. But the damage left behind on a roof, gutters, siding, and windows is often real, and the evidence starts fading right away. Knowing <strong>how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</strong> is one of the most useful skills a Dallas homeowner or business owner can have, because the insurer will decide your claim largely on what it can see and verify.</p>

<p>This guide gives you a practical, step-by-step method: what to look for, what tools to use, how to take photos and video that actually help, how to use public storm data, and how to organize it all so your claim is clear from day one. It applies to homes and commercial buildings in Dallas, Dallas County, and the surrounding North Texas communities.</p>

<h2>Why hail documentation matters so much in Dallas</h2>

<p>Dallas County is firmly in hail country. NOAA’s Storm Events Database lists 841 hail reports for the county since 1955, and 250 of them were 1.75 inches (golf ball size) or larger. Reports peak in April and May, with a long season from March through June and a smaller rise in October. Large-hail days are not rare: NOAA records include 4-inch hail <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1105878" target="_blank" rel="noopener">near Cedar Hill on June 12, 2023</a>, grapefruit-size hail in Irving on May 24, 2011, and 3-inch hail <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1326034" target="_blank" rel="noopener">near Dallas Baptist University on April 25, 2026</a>, where car windows were blown out.</p>

<p>Because storms are frequent, the same roof may have been hit more than once, and insurers often look closely at whether damage came from the storm you reported, an earlier storm, or normal aging. Strong documentation answers those questions before they are asked:</p>
<ul>
<li>It shows the damage exists and exactly where it is.</li>
<li>It shows how widespread and severe it is.</li>
<li>It connects the damage to a specific storm date rather than wear.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Texas Department of Insurance’s <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/after-hail-or-windstorms.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">advice after hail</a> is straightforward: take photos and video, list the damage inside and outside, and keep damaged items until your insurer says you can throw them away.</p>

<h2>What hail damage looks like</h2>

<h3>On the roof</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition (asphalt) shingles:</strong> random, roughly circular marks where granules are knocked off and the dark mat shows through; soft, bruised spots that feel spongy when pressed; cracked shingles or broken seal strips.</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofing and components:</strong> dents in panels, ridge vents, turtle vents, pipe jacks, flashing, and chimney caps. Note that some policies exclude cosmetic damage to metal roofing, so check yours.</li>
<li><strong>Tile and slate:</strong> cracks, chips, or broken corners, sometimes hairline.</li>
<li><strong>Low-slope commercial roofs (TPO, modified bitumen):</strong> punctures, fractures, or crushed insulation beneath the membrane, which may need closer inspection to confirm.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Around the exterior</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dents in gutters, downspouts, and metal window wraps</li>
<li>Torn or punctured window screens and cracked glass</li>
<li>Cracked or chipped siding, trim, and paint; spatter marks on fences, decks, and outdoor furniture</li>
<li>Bent fins or dents on the outdoor AC condenser</li>
<li>Dents on garage doors, mailboxes, and light fixtures</li>
</ul>

<h3>Inside and hidden</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ceiling stains, bubbling paint, or drips during later rain</li>
<li>Wet or compressed attic insulation</li>
<li>Water stains or daylight on the underside of the roof decking, seen from the attic</li>
</ul>
<p>Some damage appears right away; leaks may not show up until the next heavy rain. Treat documentation as an ongoing task for the first few weeks, not a one-day job.</p>

