<p>Professionals may also use drones, moisture meters, Xactimate estimating software, and Matterport 3D imaging. You do not need any of that to build a strong starting record.</p>

<h2>Step-by-step: how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Put safety first</h3>
<p>Do not climb onto a wet, steep, or damaged roof, and stay away from downed power lines. Two-story homes, steep pitches, and tile roofs are especially risky. Work from the ground and leave the roof surface to a qualified roofer or adjuster.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Record the storm itself</h3>
<p>Write down the date, approximate start time, how long it lasted, the largest hail you saw, and the wind direction if you noticed it. Photograph hailstones next to a ruler or coin as soon as possible, because they melt quickly. Video of hail falling or piling up in the yard is useful too.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Shoot wide, mid-range, and close-up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wide:</strong> each side of the building from the street or yard, showing the roof and that elevation.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> each roof slope you can see, each gutter run, each window, and the AC unit, so a reviewer can tell where every close-up was taken.</li>
<li><strong>Close-up:</strong> individual impacts with a tape measure or coin for scale.</li>
</ul>
<p>Take more photos than you think you need. Keep them sharp and evenly lit; low morning or late-afternoon sun can make dents easier to see on metal. Do not use filters or edits.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Mark and measure</h3>
<p>Where it is safe, circle impacts on gutters, downspouts, and siding with chalk and photograph them with a tape measure. Measuring the width of dents in soft metals gives a sense of hail size. On roofs, professionals often mark a test square on each slope and count hits, which is one reason a qualified roof inspection matters.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Check soft metals and the AC unit</h3>
<p>Gutters, downspouts, roof vents, and AC condenser fins dent easily and rarely show “wear” that looks like hail. Consistent dents on these items, facing the same direction, are strong supporting evidence for damage on the roof above them.</p>

<h3>Step 6: Document the interior and attic</h3>
<p>Photograph any ceiling stains, wet insulation, or drips. If you can safely reach the attic, look at the underside of the decking with a flashlight. Water damage changes quickly, so photograph it promptly and again if it spreads.</p>

[[cta]]

<h3>Step 7: Record a narrated video walkthrough</h3>
<p>Walk around the building slowly and describe what you see: “North side, gutter above the garage, dents about an inch wide.” State the date at the start. Video helps an adjuster understand the layout and how damage on different items relates.</p>

<h3>Step 8: Pull the storm data</h3>
<p>Search NOAA’s <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a> for Dallas County on your storm date, and check National Weather Service Dallas/Fort Worth reports. Save a copy of any report near your address. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> and <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas storm history</a> page make this easier. Keep in mind that a lack of reports near you does not prove hail did not fall; reports depend on someone observing and submitting them.</p>

<h3>Step 9: Keep a written log</h3>
<p>Note the date you discovered each piece of damage, when you reported the claim, the claim number, inspection dates, and every call or email with the insurer, including names. A simple dated note on your phone works if you keep it consistent.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Document-Hail-Damage-for-an-Insurance-Claim-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner and an adjuster in a hard hat reviewing notes on a clipboard indoors" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A written log of dates, names, and conversations keeps the claim organized when follow-up questions come.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Temporary repairs: document before and after</h2>

<p>Most policies expect you to take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or boarding a broken window. Photograph the damage first, then photograph the temporary repair, and keep every receipt. Hold off on permanent repairs until the insurer has had a chance to inspect, unless waiting would cause more damage; if so, document thoroughly and tell the insurer in writing.</p>

<h2>Organizing your claim file</h2>

<p>A tidy file makes it easier for an adjuster to say yes. Set up one folder, digital or paper, with:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photos and video:</strong> originals kept unedited, sorted by area (roof, each elevation, interior, AC, contents).</li>
<li><strong>Storm evidence:</strong> your notes, hailstone photos, and saved NOAA or NWS reports.</li>
<li><strong>Policy documents:</strong> the full policy, declarations page, and endorsements. Note your wind/hail deductible, which on many Texas policies is a percentage of the dwelling limit.</li>
<li><strong>Estimates and invoices:</strong> contractor bids, temporary repair receipts, and the insurer’s estimate when it arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Correspondence log:</strong> dates, names, and summaries of every contact.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lays this out in a format you can print. For roof-specific advice, see <a href="/hail-damage-roof-insurance-claim-how-to-protect-your-roof/">hail damage roof insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Common documentation mistakes</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Only close-ups.</strong> Without wide and mid-range shots, no one can tell where a dent is or how widespread the damage is.</li>
<li><strong>No scale reference.</strong> A dent without a coin or tape can look smaller or ambiguous.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping soft metals.</strong> Gutters, vents, and the AC unit often provide the clearest proof.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting weeks.</strong> Fresh marks weather, and new storms can muddy the timeline.</li>
<li><strong>Editing photos.</strong> Filters and crops can raise questions; keep originals.</li>
<li><strong>Throwing away damaged items</strong> before the insurer has seen them or said it is OK.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When your documentation meets resistance</h2>

<p>Even a well-documented claim can be underpaid or denied, often on grounds like wear and tear, pre-existing damage, or “cosmetic only.” If that happens, ask for the reasons in writing, compare the insurer’s estimate to your evidence and contractor bids, and consider a second opinion. Our guide on <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-texas/">what to do if your hail claim was denied in Texas</a> walks through next steps, and <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims-in-texas/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a> explains who represents whom.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Document-Hail-Damage-for-an-Insurance-Claim-5-1024x683.webp" alt="Family on a sofa reviewing claim paperwork with an adjuster" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reviewing the insurer’s estimate against your own photos and notes can show what was missed.</figcaption></figure>

<p>A public adjuster, such as Dallas Public Adjuster, can re-inspect the property, build a line-item estimate, and present the documentation to the insurer on your behalf. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. No one can guarantee the outcome, but a complete record gives your claim the best footing. Learn more on our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">Dallas hail damage claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>Final thoughts</h2>

<p>Hail damage rarely looks dramatic, but it can be costly. The owners who come through the claims process with the least frustration tend to document early, document thoroughly, and keep everything organized. If you would like a second set of eyes on your photos, estimate, or denial letter, Dallas Public Adjuster serves Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> and we can talk through where your claim stands. For legal questions, talk with a Texas attorney.</p>
